    <div>
      <h2 style="font-size:1.8rem;padding-top:0.5rem;">What Makes B2B Marketing ROI Different</h2>
      <p>B2B marketing ROI is fundamentally different from B2C for three reasons:</p>
      <ol style="margin-bottom:1.5rem;">
        <li><strong style="color:#fff;">Long sales cycles:</strong> A B2B deal that closes in Q4 may have originated from a marketing touch in Q1. Simple last-click attribution will systematically under-credit marketing.</li>
        <li><strong style="color:#fff;">Multi-stakeholder buying:</strong> A typical B2B deal involves 6-10 decision-makers. Attributing revenue to a single touch understates marketing's role in influencing the buying committee.</li>
        <li><strong style="color:#fff;">Revenue concentration:</strong> 20% of customers often generate 80% of revenue. A single large deal can swing your entire ROI calculation, making trend lines essential.</li>
      </ol>

      <h2 style="font-size:1.8rem;margin-bottom:1rem;padding-top:0.5rem;">B2B Marketing ROI Formula</h2>
      <p>The basic formula is straightforward. The complexity is in defining "revenue attributed to marketing" correctly:</p>
      <div style="background:#0a1628;border:1px solid rgba(201,168,76,0.25);border-radius:12px;padding:1.5rem;margin-bottom:1.5rem;text-align:center;">
        <div style="color:#C9A84C;font-size:1.25rem;font-family:Georgia,serif;font-weight:700;">Marketing ROI = ((Revenue from Marketing - Marketing Spend) / Marketing Spend) x 100</div>
        <div style="color:rgba(255,255,255,0.5);font-size:0.8rem;margin-top:0.75rem;">Example: ($500K revenue - $100K spend) / $100K spend x 100 = 400% ROI</div>
      </div>
      <p>For B2B, apply gross margin to revenue before calculating ROI. Using gross revenue inflates the number. Use gross profit (revenue x gross margin %) for an accurate picture.</p>

      <h2 style="font-size:1.8rem;margin-bottom:1rem;padding-top:0.5rem;">B2B Marketing ROI Benchmarks by Segment (2025)</h2>
